<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Go-Kameyama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1424">1424</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Go-Kameyama (died 1424) was the fourth and last emperor of the Southern Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, reigning from 1383 to 1392. Militarily weakened, he accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>'s offer to reunite the two courts, handing the imperial regalia to the Northern emperor Go-Komatsu on the promise that the two lines would alternate on the throne. The promise was never kept.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-go-kameyama.jpg" alt="Emperor Go-Kameyama" width="320" height="530" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by メディアックス(Mediax) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Go-Kameyama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Emperor Go-Kameyama, whose personal name was Hironari, was born around 1347 and is counted as the 99th emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the traditional order of succession. He was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-murakami">Emperor Go-Murakami</a> and Fujiwara Katsuko, better known as Kaki Mon'in. Little is known of his empress or other consorts. His empress was a daughter of Kitabatake Akinobu, and Imperial Prince Tsuneatsu is believed to have been his son. His name recalls the 13th-century Emperor Kameyama, with go meaning later, so he could also be called Kameyama the second.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Go-Kameyama came to the throne during the Nanboku-chō period, when rival claimants to the Chrysanthemum Throne gathered supporters in a Northern court and a Southern court. He became emperor of the Southern court in 1383, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-chokei">Emperor Chōkei</a> abdicated. On 15 October 1392, at the insistence of the peace faction among his own courtiers, he applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> for peace. He then returned to the capital and handed the Sacred Treasures to his Northern court rival, and in doing so he was understood to have abdicated.</p><p>Under the terms of the peace the Northern and Southern courts were supposed to alternate control of the throne. In 1412 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-komatsu">Emperor Go-Komatsu</a> reneged by abdicating in favour of his own son, and no Southern court claimant sat on the throne again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his abdication Go-Kameyama went into seclusion, but in 1410 he returned to Yoshino. He died on 10 May 1424, and the Imperial Household Agency recognises Saga no ogura no misasagi, in Ukyō-ku, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, as his tomb.</p><p>Since 1911 the Japanese government has declared the southern claimants to have been the rightful emperors, on the reasoning that the Southern court kept the three sacred treasures. This holds even though every later emperor, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a>, descended from the Northern court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Go-Kameyama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Go-Kameyama</b>, the 99th emperor in the traditional order of succession and the last of the Southern Court, gave up the throne to his rival Go-Komatsu.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its emperors after Go-Daigo were Go-Murakami, Chokei and <b>Go-Kameyama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-court">Southern Court</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Daikaku-ji Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.03,135.68&amp;year=1414" aria-label="Daikaku-ji Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7988 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Emperor Go-Kameyama"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8438 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8438 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.03,135.68&amp;year=1414">What was here in 1414? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
